WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.180
A practical concurrency safety baseline for C# starts with shared mutable state.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:13.240
Identify what can change, who owns it, which operations can run at the same time, and which invariants must remain true.

3
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:20.780
Minimize sharing through ownership, immutability, local scope, snapshots, and message-based designs where they fit.

4
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.540
Use synchronization intentionally.

5
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.320
A lock should protect a clear critical section.

6
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.140
A semaphore should limit a clear resource.

7
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.960
A concurrent collection should match the workflow instead of becoming a shortcut around design.

8
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:41.380
Prefer the simplest primitive that preserves correctness and can be reviewed by the team that maintains the code.

9
00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.060
Use async and await with discipline.

10
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:54.880
Prefer async all the way through supported call chains, avoid blocking on tasks, observe exceptions, define cancellation behavior, and avoid unmanaged fire-and-forget work.

11
00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:00.020
Async code still needs ownership, lifecycle, and safe access to shared state.

12
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.100
Bound parallelism and background work.

13
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:16.800
Control fan-out, respect dependency capacity, avoid leaking request-scoped services into unmanaged work, pass cancellation tokens, set timeouts, and define cleanup or retry behavior when work stops halfway through.

14
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.800
Finally, make concurrency observable.

15
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:31.260
Log safe correlation and failure context, monitor queue growth, timeouts, lock contention, thread pool pressure, duplicate work, and missing updates.

16
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.200
Review concurrency behavior under production-like load when feasible.

17
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:42.020
The goal is code that remains correct when real systems overlap, slow down, retry, and shut down.
